          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "If you have severe thirst, frequent urination with confusion, vomiting, or drowsiness, seek urgent medical care now rather than waiting for an appointment.\n\nType 2 diabetes is a long-term condition in which the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, the hormone that moves sugar from the blood into cells. Over time the pancreas may not keep up with the extra demand, so blood glucose stays above the normal range. It usually develops gradually, and many people have no symptoms when it is found (General clinical reference, *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*).\n\nThe diagnosis code is **E11** in ICD-10-CM, in Chapter 4 for endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*). ICD-10-CM groups diabetes by cause, not treatment, and a record that says only \"diabetes mellitus\" without a type defaults to type 2. Using insulin does not change the code; insulin use is recorded separately. My search tool did not independently confirm the code, so your clinician or health records can verify it.\n\nI could not find what current guidance recommends as first-line medication or lifestyle therapy for newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes. The guidance I retrieved covered other conditions, so I have not filled that gap from memory.\n\nThe retrieved guidance does give a glucose target. Diabetes association standards (*Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*) describe an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults. The target is individualized: a stricter goal, such as below 6.5 percent, may be considered if it can be reached without significant low blood sugar, and a looser goal may suit some people.\n\nThe two bodies also disagree on screening. Diabetes association guidance describes screening all adults from age 35, regardless of weight. Preventive-services guidance limits routine screening to adults aged 35 to 70 who are overweight or obese (*Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds*). This concerns screening, not treatment.\n\nQuestions to bring to your clinician:\n- What is my HbA1c, and what target has been set for me?\n- Which first-line treatment is recommended for me, and why?\n- Which lifestyle changes are most useful in my case, and is there a structured program available?\n- Which diagnosis code will appear on my records?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
